//@ normative
//@ ok
import { HashMap, map } from hashmap::std;
import { Collection, coll } from collection::std;

fun main() <noret> {
   let a <HashMap<string, int>> = map!{
	"alex" => 10,
	"robot" => 20,
   }; // Macros can handle syntax/node. dict like macro

   let b <Collection<int>> = coll![1,2,3,4,5]; // List like macro

   let r <string> = format!("{}", b[2]); // funccall like macro
}

// Promoted, ADR 0023 steps 8 and 9. The two `Undefined macro` diagnostics this file carried --
// `map!` at 7:35 and `coll!` at 12:30 -- are gone. Step 8 wrote the two macros into the two
// libraries that own the types they build: `@macro map(p)` at the end of lib/std/hashmap.fin
// and `@macro coll(p)` at the end of lib/std/collection.fin, each one line, each handing its
// bracket to the `from_prototype` the library already declared. That works because the bracket
// carries the shape: `{...}` arrives as one prototype with the written keys and `[...]` as one
// keyed `0..n-1`, which are exactly the two `from_prototype` signatures, so neither macro
// constructs anything -- each is a rename.
//
// One repair went with it, on lines 3 and 4. The file called `map!` and `coll!` bare while
// importing only `HashMap` and `Collection`, and a named import carries the names it names: a
// macro has to be asked for. So `map` and `coll` join the two type names in the same braces.
// That is the documented rule and not a workaround -- docs/guide/11-macros-and-preprocessor.md
// says a bare call after a whole-module import reports `Undefined macro` for the same reason --
// and it is the second repair to these two lines, the first being the imports themselves. This
// prose sits at the end of the file rather than under the expectation because
// lib/std/hashmap.fin, lib/std/collection.fin and the note below all cite this file by line,
// and inserting at the top would have staled every one of them at once.
//
// What the two calls do at runtime is still nothing: `from_prototype` drops its entries in both
// libraries, because a prototype resolves no member and no index so nothing in the language can
// walk one. Line 7 types as a `HashMap<string, int>` and line 12 as a `Collection<int>`, and
// both are empty. That is a gap in the libraries and not in this file, which is why the
// expectation is `ok` -- ADR 0008 makes an expectation a claim about diagnostics.
//
// Line 14's `format!("{}", b[2])` resolved one step earlier, under ADR 0023 step 6, and lowers
// as of step 7. The note this replaced recorded that, and recorded the count falling from five
// to three to two; it read, in full:
//
//   "Two diagnostics, both of them one missing feature. `map!{ ... }` at 7:35 -- "Undefined macro 'map!'"; the brace and bracket macro-call forms parse now on both sides of an `=`, and nothing defines `map!` or `coll!` -- `coll![1,2,3,4,5]` on 12 reports the same way, and both wait on the declarations ADR 0023 step 8 adds to `lib/std/hashmap.fin` and `lib/std/collection.fin`. The count fell from three when ADR 0023 step 6 landed the builtin macro table: `format!("{}", b[2])` on 14 used to report with the other two and now resolves, because `format!` is a macro the compiler implements rather than one a file has to define. The two imports on 3 and 4 are a repair. The file used `HashMap<string, int>` on 7 and `Collection<int>` on 12 with no import at all and reported "Undefined type" for both, and both names are declared `pub` in the shipped library -- `pub struct HashMap<T, U>` at lib/std/hashmap.fin:45 and `pub struct Collection<T>` at lib/std/collection.fin:47 -- so the two names were the file's omission and not a gap in the compiler. The spelling is the corpus's own: prototype_test.fin:3-4 writes these two lines verbatim for these two types, and importing.fin:14, deeptest4.fin:3 and stdlib/hashmap.fin:4 write them one at a time. The count fell from five to three and nothing new appeared -- neither library reports a diagnostic of its own through the import, which is worth recording because a blind import does not always behave that way"
